  {
   "id": "T5",
   "title": "Business resilience",
   "target": "Close about half of the five year survival gap to England, on pooled pairs of birth cohorts: from 7.7 points (2019 and 2020 cohorts) to 4 points or fewer (2030 and 2031 cohorts). Separately, hold wholesale and retail output at or above £523m, its 2024 level.",
   "baseline": "30.2% five year survival, 225 of the 745 businesses born in Burnley in 2019 and 2020 (single cohorts 27.5% and 33.3%)",
   "comparator": "England 37.9% on the same basis; the fourteen Lancashire areas 38.4%",
   "arithmetic": "England's 37.9% minus Burnley's 30.2% is a gap of 7.7 points. Half of it is about 3.9. Whole points are the finest step the data can bear, so the target is a gap of 4 points or fewer. If England stays at 37.9%, that means Burnley at about 34% or more. Counts are rounded to 5 and a pair of cohorts is about 750 births, so whole points are the finest step the target can bear.",
   "indicator": "ONS business demography each November (the 2031 cohort's five year figure comes in November 2037); three year survival on pooled cohorts as the interim check; regional GVA each autumn",
   "kind": "target",
